## Why This Is Asked

This question probes data governance, access control, and incident response fundamentals for beginner CISSP candidates, with a realistic data-import scenario.

## Key Concepts

- Data classification and handling
- RBAC/ABAC and just-in-time access
- Data provenance and SBOM concepts
- Encryption at rest and in transit; KMS/key rotation
- Immutable audit logging and incident response/testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Validate payload schema and attach a signed attestation
function validateAndSign(payload, signer) {
  // simple schema check
  if (!payload || !payload.data) throw new Error('Invalid');
  const attestation = signer.sign(JSON.stringify(payload));
  return { payload, attestation };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test end-to-end import validation in CI/CD?
- How should failed attestations be handled and remediated?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests threat modeling and practical controls for log integrity across cloud boundaries, a common fintech risk.

## Key Concepts
- Log integrity, STRIDE/PASTA, SBOM, log signing, KMS, append-only storage, WORM, cross-cloud replication, SIEM/SOAR, tabletop exercises

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo code for signing log entries
function signLogEntry(entry, key) {
  const signature = crypto.sign("SHA256", Buffer.from(JSON.stringify(entry)), key);
  return { entry, signature };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify integrity of logs during audits across clouds?
- How would you coordinate key rotation and revocation in a distributed SIEM?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical experience architecting cross-cloud identity solutions, zero-trust controls, and incident readiness for a high-stakes data platform.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-cloud identity federation (IBM Cloud, Coinbase Cloud, Two Sigma)
- STRIDE/PASTA threat modeling methodology
- OAuth2/OIDC token lifecycle management with HSM signing
- Phishing-resistant MFA (FIDO2/WebAuthn, hardware tokens)
- Device attestation and mutual TLS
- RBAC/ABAC hybrid access control model
- Tamper-evident audit trails
- Incident response and testing: tabletop exercises, red-team drills

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo token validation sketch
async function verifyToken(token, publicKey) {
  try {
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, publicKey, {
      algorithms: ['RS256', 'ES256'],
      issuer: 'https://idp.example.com',
      audience: 'data-science-platform'
    });

    // Check token age
    const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
    if (payload.exp - now > 900) { // Max 15 min
      throw new Error('Token lifetime exceeded policy');
    }

    // Verify device attestation claim
    if (!payload.device_trusted) {
      throw new Error('Device not attested');
    }

    return payload;
  } catch (err) {
    throw new AuthenticationError(err.message);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes crypto governance at scale, bridging cloud-native KMS with on-prem HSMs, PKI, and policy-driven agility. It tests risk framing, regulatory considerations, and incident response readiness.

## Key Concepts
- Crypto agility and policy-driven key selection
- HSMs, PKI, certificate management
- Key lifecycle, rotation, retirement
- Access control, least privilege, and audits
- Post-quantum readiness and algorithm agility

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: select allowed algorithm per policy
const policy = getCryptoPolicy();
function selectAlgorithm(payload){
  for (const a of policy.allowedAlgorithms){
    if (canUse(a, payload)) return a;
  }
  throw new Error('No suitable algorithm available');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate crypto policy changes without interrupting live traffic?
- What are trade-offs between cloud KMS and on-prem HSMs for encryption at rest vs in transit in high-load fintech workflows?
